# vcl_miss and vcl_pass: sign the origin request to GCS. Rendered by
# Terraform (fastly.tf) with the HMAC key of the kiewer-fastly service
# account (storage.tf), which has read on both buckets.
#
# GCS accepts an HMAC key with the legacy AWS signature, the scheme
# Fastly's own GCS guide uses:
#   Authorization: AWS <access id>:<base64(hmac-sha1(secret, string to sign))>
#   string to sign = <method> LF <Content-MD5> LF <Content-Type> LF <Date> LF <resource>
# Content-MD5 and Content-Type are empty on a read; Date is the header set
# just before; the resource is the path-style URL, bucket included, which
# recv already made the request URL. The method signed is bereq.method,
# the one the origin sees: a HEAD becomes a GET on a miss (Fastly fetches
# the body to cache it) and stays a HEAD on a pass.
if (req.http.X-Kiewer-Origin == "gcs") {
  set bereq.http.Date = now;
  set bereq.http.Authorization = "AWS ${access_id}:" digest.hmac_sha1_base64("${secret}", bereq.method LF LF LF bereq.http.Date LF bereq.url.path);
  # Blobs are stored gzip with Content-Encoding: gzip (ADR 0002). Asking
  # for gzip keeps GCS from transcoding, so the cached object is the
  # stored bytes and the Content-Encoding reaches the browser.
  set bereq.http.Accept-Encoding = "gzip";
  # Nothing of the client's reaches the bucket.
  unset bereq.http.Cookie;
  unset bereq.http.X-Forwarded-Host;
  unset bereq.http.X-Kiewer-Origin;
}
